                <pb facs="0235" n="123" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg123"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/1541: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-26T12:00:00+03:00">April 26,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 26—8:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>159. Reference my 157, April 25, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="26" xml:id="d104-f1"/> and
                    despatch No. 540, April 28 [<hi rendition="#italic">23</hi>], Tass report dated
                    yesterday stated that at General <pb facs="0236" n="124" xml:id="pg124"/>
                    Congress of Sinkiang representatives held at Bakhty on April 10th, to celebrate
                    second anniversary of formation of new provincial government, the chairman of
                    the Provincial Council “Governor of the Province” announced that his government
                    was completely convinced that the Soviet Union had no aggressive designs on
                    Sinkiang and that complete friendly relations existed between the two
                    states.</p>
                <p>The report further states that the Sinkiang Congress transmitted a telegram to
                    Nanking reiterating its loyalty to the Nanking Government and asking for further
                    cooperation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
